<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C4.S1 — Entropy of entanglement of a two-parameter state</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C4.S1 — Schmidt coefficients and entropy</p></div><p>Let \(|\psi\rangle=\cos\theta\,|00\rangle+\sin\theta\,|11\rangle\) with \(\theta\in(0,\pi/2)\).</p><p>(a) Give the larger eigenvalue of the reduced state \(\rho_A\) (assume \(\theta\in(0,\pi/4)\)).</p><p>\(\lambda_{\max}=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the entropy of entanglement in bits, as a function of <code>theta</code>. Use <code>log(x)/log(2)</code> for \(\log_2 x\).</p><p>\(E=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The state is already in Schmidt form with \(\lambda_1=\cos\theta\), \(\lambda_2=\sin\theta\), so \(\rho_A=\mathrm{diag}(\cos^2\theta,\sin^2\theta)\).</p><p>(a) For \(\theta\in(0,\pi/4)\), \(\cos^2\theta>\sin^2\theta\), so \(\lambda_{\max}=\cos^2\theta\).</p><p>(b) \(E=-\cos^2\theta\log_2\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta\log_2\sin^2\theta\).</p><p>Check the limits: \(\theta\to0\) gives a product state and \(E\to0\); \(\theta=\pi/4\) gives the Bell state and \(E=1\) ebit. Since local unitaries cannot change the Schmidt coefficients, \(E\) is invariant under \(U_A\otimes U_B\), as any entanglement measure must be.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber></idnumber>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2024010100</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>ta1 : cos(theta)^2;
ta2 : -cos(theta)^2*log(cos(theta)^2)/log(2) - sin(theta)^2*log(sin(theta)^2)/log(2);</text>
    </questionvariables>
    <specificfeedback format="html">
