  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C4.S3 — Resource identities for teleportation and dense coding</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C4.S3 — Counting resources</p></div><p>(a) Teleporting one qubit consumes one ebit and how many classical bits?</p><p>cbits \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Superdense coding transmits how many classical bits per transmitted qubit, given one shared ebit?</p><p>cbits \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Alice and Bob share \(n={@n@}\) Bell pairs. Give the maximum number of classical bits Alice can convey to Bob by sending \(n\) qubits using dense coding.</p><p>bits \(=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Two: the Bell measurement has four equally likely outcomes, so Alice must send \(\log_2 4=2\) bits. Fewer would allow superluminal signalling, since Bob\'s marginal is \(I/2\) until he learns the outcome.</p><p>(b) Two, by the Bennett–Wiesner protocol: the four Bell states are perfectly distinguishable and are reached by \(I,X,Z,XZ\) acting on one half.</p><p>(c) \(2n=2\cdot{@n@}={@ta3@}\) bits. This is optimal: the Holevo bound caps the accessible information at \(2n\) bits for \(n\) transmitted qubits assisted by entanglement.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
